Here are a few ways to center a div in CSS, depending on what kind of centering you need:

**1. Flexbox (horizontal + vertical centering — most common)**
```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  height: 100vh;           /* or whatever height */
}
```

**2. Grid (horizontal + vertical)**
```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
}
```

**3. Horizontal centering only**
```css
.child {
  margin: 0 auto;
  width: fit-content; /* or a fixed width */
}
```

**4. Absolute positioning (centered in relative parent)**
```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
}
.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

Flexbox is usually the go-to — it's flexible, well-supported, and easy to reason about.
